          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p2\",\"quote\":\"Exploration of Titan began with NASA 's Pioneer 11 encounter with the Saturn system in 1979, followed by the Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 flybys in 1980 and 1981. These missions confirmed Titan's dense atmosphere while Voyager 1 showed that a thick haze obscures the surface. The Cassini–Huygens mission conducted the most extensive exploration of Titan to date, with Cassini making 127 close flybys between 2004 and 2017.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p3\",\"quote\":\"Future exploration is planned with NASA's Dragonfly mission, a rotorcraft lander scheduled for launch in 2028 and arrival in 2034.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p8\",\"quote\":\"The first spacecraft to explore Titan was Pioneer 11 , which flew past the moon at a distance of approximately 354,000 km (220,000 mi) on 2 September 1979 during its encounter with Saturn.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p12\",\"quote\":\"The close encounter of Voyager 1 enabled detailed measurements of Titan's mass, radius, atmospheric density, composition, temperature, and surface pressure.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p13\",\"quote\":\"The dense atmosphere prevented both Voyager spacecraft from directly observing Titan's surface. Images showed an orange globe completely obscured by atmospheric haze, although the spacecraft detected a bluish haze layer at higher altitudes.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p15\",\"quote\":\"Cassini immediately began observing the moon using radar and infrared instruments capable of penetrating its dense atmospheric haze.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p16\",\"quote\":\"A major achievement of the mission occurred on 14 January 2005, when the European-built Huygens probe descended through Titan's atmosphere and landed on the surface.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p17\",\"quote\":\"Over the following 13 years, Cassini conducted 127 close flybys of Titan, including its closest encounter in 2010, when it passed about 880 km (550 mi) above Titan's surface.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p22\",\"quote\":\"Huygens also obtained the first direct measurements of Titan's lower atmosphere.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p23\",\"quote\":\"The rotorcraft will fly between multiple locations on Titan's surface, collecting samples and studying a wide range of geological environments.\"}]}"
        }
